    <exercise number="35" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The terms of a series are defined recursively by the equations</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$a_{1} = 2 a_{n + 1} = \frac{5n + 1}{4n + 3}a_{n}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Determine whether $$\sum a_{n}$$ converges or diverges.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>D</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="36" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A series $$\sum a_{n}$$ is defined by the equations</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$a_{1} = 1 a_{n + 1} = \frac{2 + \cos n}{\sqrt{n}}a_{n}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Determine whether $$\sum a_{n}$$ converges or diverges.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>37 and 38 Let $$\left\{ b_{n} \right\}$$ be a sequence of positive numbers that converges to $$\frac{1}{2}$$ . Determine whether the given series is absolutely convergent.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="37" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{b_{n}^{n}\cos n\pi}{n}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$AC$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="38" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{\left( {- 1} \right)^{n}n!}{n^{n}b_{1}b_{2}b_{3}\cdots b_{n}}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="39" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>For which of the following series is the Ratio Test inconclusive (that is, it fails to give a definite answer)?</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{3}}$$</item>
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{n}{2^{n}}$$</item>
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{\left( {- 3} \right)^{n - 1}}{\sqrt{n}}$$</item>
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{\sqrt{n}}{1 + n^{2}}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>(a) and (d)</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="40" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>For which positive integers $$k$$ is the following series convergent?</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{\left( {n!} \right)^{2}}{\left( {kn} \right)!}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="41" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Show that $$\sum_{n = 0}^{\infty}x^{n} \slash n!$$ converges for all $$x$$ .</item>
          <item>Deduce that $$\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}x^{n} \slash n! = 0$$ for all $$x$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="42" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Let $$\sum a_{n}$$ be a series with positive terms and let $$r_{n} = a_{n + 1} \slash a_{n}$$ . Suppose that $$\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}r_{n} = L &lt; 1$$ , so $$\sum a_{n}$$ converges by the Ratio Test. As usual, we let $$R_{n}$$ be the remainder after $$n$$ terms, that is,</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$R_{n} = a_{n + 1} + a_{n + 2} + a_{n + 3} + \cdots$$</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>If $$\left\{ r_{n} \right\}$$ is a decreasing sequence and $$r_{n + 1} &lt; 1$$ , show, by summing a geometric series, that $$R_{n} \leqslant \frac{a_{n + 1}}{1 - r_{n + 1}}$$</item>
          <item>If $$\left\{ r_{n} \right\}$$ is an increasing sequence, show that $$R_{n} \leqslant \frac{a_{n + 1}}{1 - L}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="43" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Find the partial sum $$s_{5}$$ of the series $$\sum_{n = 1}^{\infty}1 \slash \left( {n2^{n}} \right)$$ . Use Exercise 42 to estimate the error in using $$s_{5}$$ as an approximation to the sum of the series.</item>
          <item>Find a value of $$n$$ so that $$s_{n}$$ is within $$0.00005$$ of the sum. Use this value of $$n$$ to approximate the sum of the series.</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\frac{661}{960} \approx 0.68854$$ , $${error} &lt; 0.00521$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$n \geqslant 11$$ , $$0.693109$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="44" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the sum of the first $$10$$ terms to approximate the sum of the series</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{n}{2^{n}}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Use Exercise 42 to estimate the error.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="45" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Prove the Root Test. [Hint for part (i): Take any number $$r$$ such that $$L &lt; r &lt; 1$$ and use the fact that there is an integer $$N$$ such that $$\sqrt[n]{\left| a_{n} \right|^{}} &lt; r$$ whenever $$n \geqslant N$$ .]</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="46" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Around 1910, the Indian mathematician Srinivasa Ramanujan discovered the formula</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\frac{1}{\pi} = \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{9801}\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{\infty}\frac{\left( {4n} \right)!\left( {1103 + 26390n} \right)}{\left( {n!} \right)^{4}396^{4n}}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>William Gosper used this series in 1985 to compute the first $$17$$ million digits of $$\pi$$ .</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Verify that the series is convergent.</item>
          <item>How many correct decimal places of $$\pi$$ do you get if you use just the first term of the series? What if you use two terms?</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
